## Three fixes (each at its owner; no new timers/state; report rule count before/after)
1. Framework host lifecycle: the Nest integration ends with the host `stop()` (transport only) instead of `shutdown()`, leaving
   User/Instance Spot timers alive while the runtime reports Stopped → the process never exits. Make the Nest shutdown hook call the
   host's `shutdown()` (the single owner of teardown per 05-host-relocation-flow §14: Stopped only after all owned resources are
   released); remove any duplicate partial-stop path so there is one shutdown owner. Regression: a contract test that after
   `app.close()` no framework timers/handles remain (`process._getActiveHandles()` filtered to framework-owned) and the process can
   exit.
2. ZoneWorld Ops: the heartbeat control send uses the binding's sync `submit_sync()` (Core NONE) on the JS main thread, blocking the
   SIGINT handler while admission waits. Use the binding async terminal (same owner as the deliver fix `81a1f6fbfc`); no framework
   retry/timer. Regression: Ops exits on SIGINT while heartbeat admission is pending.
3. Sample runner: GameQuest's owner-loss scenario SIGKILLs the owner intentionally (`run_sample.sh`/scenario), and the runner's
   cleanup then counts that already-killed role as a teardown failure. Follow the java runner semantics (reap the intentional kill
   in the scenario step; only roles still alive at cleanup count) — one rule for "who is a teardown failure". Keep the SIGKILL
   reporting from 9a0371fea7 for every other role.

## Verify
All 7 node samples individually (exit 0, no SIGKILLed role) + the aggregate `bash samples/run_samples.sh` once; `npm run typecheck`,
lint on touched files, touched contract tests, `npm test` once.

